/* ============================================================
   DEATH GHOST — derezz scan line cascade fix
   ============================================================
   User report May-1: "I still see the death scan line as blue
   even after the theme fix."

   The fix at style.css:5874 set background + box-shadow via
   --derezz-color, but it was being overridden by the diagonal
   sheen rule at line 16003 (.card.hand-card::after,
   .card.ally-card::after, .card.enemy-card::after) which has
   specificity (0,0,2,1) and comes LATER in the cascade than
   the death-ghost rule at line 5874 (specificity 0,0,1,1).

   The earlier .card-death-ghost.ally-card::after at line 5944
   matched specificity (0,0,2,1) but still came BEFORE the
   sheen rule in the cascade, so it lost the tie-break.

   Solution: append the override here, at the very end of the
   file, so the cascade-order tie resolves in our favor. The
   sheen's diagonal blue gradient is suppressed (background:
   none) and only the thin derezz line remains, painted in the
   active player/enemy theme color. */
.card-death-ghost::after {
  background: var(--derezz-color, rgba(var(--enemy-r, 231), var(--enemy-g, 76), var(--enemy-b, 60), 1)) !important;
  background-image: none !important;  /* suppress the diagonal sheen */
  inset: auto !important;              /* override the sheen rule's inset:0 */
  top: 100% !important;
  left: -4px !important;
  right: -4px !important;
  height: 2px !important;
  border-radius: 0 !important;
  opacity: 1 !important;                /* sheen sets opacity:0 by default */
  box-shadow:
    0 0 10px var(--derezz-color, rgba(var(--enemy-r, 231), var(--enemy-g, 76), var(--enemy-b, 60), 0.95)),
    0 0 20px var(--derezz-color, rgba(var(--enemy-r, 231), var(--enemy-g, 76), var(--enemy-b, 60), 0.55)) !important;
  animation: derezzLine 0.7s linear forwards !important;
}
.card-death-ghost.ally-card::after {
  --derezz-color: rgba(var(--player-r, 79), var(--player-g, 195), var(--player-b, 247), 1) !important;
}
.card-death-ghost.enemy-card::after {
  --derezz-color: rgba(var(--enemy-r, 231), var(--enemy-g, 76), var(--enemy-b, 60), 1) !important;
}

/* ============================================================
   TRON FLARE PASS — visual polish wave (research-driven)
   ============================================================
   Sources: GMUNK Tron Legacy work, Cyberpunk 2077 HUD, Marvel
   Snap "piano glass," CRT shader patterns. Dialed for THIS
   game's existing language (theme-rgb tokens, Tron neon vocab).
   Each block is gated to play nice with `prefers-reduced-motion`.
   ============================================================ */

/* ---- #10 CRT scanline overlay (always-on, faint) ----
   2px repeating-gradient drawn on html::before so it sits above
   every layer including modals. mix-blend-mode: overlay so it
   composites with content rather than dimming it. Opacity ~5%
   at idle, intensifies via .crt-burst class for one-shot beats
   (card death, big damage, phase wipes). pointer-events: none. */
html::before {
  content: "";
  position: fixed;
  inset: 0;
  z-index: 99996;
  pointer-events: none;
  background:
    repeating-linear-gradient(
      0deg,
      rgba(255,255,255,0.045) 0 1px,
      transparent 1px 3px);
  mix-blend-mode: overlay;
  opacity: 1;
  transition: opacity 0.4s ease;
}
@media (prefers-reduced-motion: reduce) {
  html::before { display: none; }
}
/* Card-death burst — bumps scanline opacity briefly. The
   burst class is added to body by ui.js _onCardDeath hook
   when wired; pure CSS for now — animates from 1 to ~3 to 1. */
@keyframes crtBurst {
  0%, 100% { opacity: 1; }
  20%      { opacity: 3; }
  60%      { opacity: 1.6; }
}
body.crt-burst::before { animation: crtBurst 380ms ease-out; }

/* ---- #5 HUD pill broadcast scanline — REMOVED ----
   Earlier draft added a sweeping 1px line on .hud-pill-main /
   .hud-round / .hud-count plus `overflow: hidden` to clip it.
   The overflow clipped the round-number diamond's corners and
   the theme-tinted line read as a stray blue highlight behind
   the AI-red diamond. User report: "in the top pill you can
   kinda see, like, a blue highlight behind the diamond, and it
   kinda looks weird." Pulled the rule entirely. The CRT overlay
   on html::before already provides ambient broadcast feel
   without artifacting on the round badge. */

/* ---- #9 Lane depth gradient (active lane rises, idle lanes sink) ----
   Each lane gets a faint radial inset on idle that suggests it
   sinks slightly into the screen. The active-lane (mid-combat)
   loses the inset and reads as "raised toward the viewer." */
.lane {
  position: relative;
}
.lane::before {
  /* Existing .lane::before may already exist as a beam-of-light
     wash; if it does, this rule's box-shadow combines additively
     since browsers stack inset shadows. */
  background-image:
    radial-gradient(ellipse 80% 60% at 50% 50%,
      rgba(0,0,0,0.0) 0%,
      rgba(0,0,0,0.18) 60%,
      rgba(0,0,0,0.32) 100%);
  pointer-events: none;
  transition: opacity 0.3s ease;
}
.lane[data-active="true"]::before,
.state-combat .lane._activeLane::before {
  opacity: 0.35;
}

/* ---- #4 Projection-base glow under board cards ----
   Trapezoidal beam emanating from the lane floor up under each
   card on the board — reads as "this card is being holographically
   projected from the lane." Theme-tinted, pulses gently. */
.lane > .card.tron-perimeter-card::after {
  content: "";
  position: absolute;
  left: 50%; bottom: -22px;
  transform: translateX(-50%);
  width: 70%; height: 24px;
  background: radial-gradient(ellipse 100% 100% at 50% 0%,
    rgba(var(--theme-rgb, 79,195,247), 0.22) 0%,
    rgba(var(--theme-rgb, 79,195,247), 0.10) 35%,
    transparent 70%);
  pointer-events: none;
  filter: blur(4px);
  opacity: 0.65;
  z-index: -1;
}
.lane > .card.player-card.tron-perimeter-card::after {
  background: radial-gradient(ellipse 100% 100% at 50% 0%,
    rgba(var(--player-r, 79), var(--player-g, 195), var(--player-b, 247), 0.28) 0%,
    rgba(var(--player-r, 79), var(--player-g, 195), var(--player-b, 247), 0.10) 35%,
    transparent 70%);
}
.lane > .card.enemy-card.tron-perimeter-card::after {
  bottom: auto; top: -22px;
  background: radial-gradient(ellipse 100% 100% at 50% 100%,
    rgba(var(--enemy-r, 231), var(--enemy-g, 76), var(--enemy-b, 60), 0.28) 0%,
    rgba(var(--enemy-r, 231), var(--enemy-g, 76), var(--enemy-b, 60), 0.10) 35%,
    transparent 70%);
}

/* ---- #2 Hexagonal lane separator caps ----
   Tron Legacy used hex motifs throughout (costumes, vehicles,
   environment). The lane vertical separator gradient terminates
   in a hex-edge marker at top + bottom — small, ~6px hexagons
   in the active lane theme. Pure CSS via clip-path. */
.lane:not(:last-child)::after {
  /* existing ::after may be in use; if so this stacks. We use
     a pseudo on the SEPARATOR rather than the lane to avoid
     collision. Capped via :not(:last-child) so the rightmost
     lane doesn't sprout a ghost separator. */
}
.lane-hex-cap {
  position: absolute;
  width: 8px; height: 8px;
  background: rgba(var(--theme-rgb, 79,195,247), 0.55);
  clip-path: polygon(25% 0%, 75% 0%, 100% 50%, 75% 100%, 25% 100%, 0% 50%);
  pointer-events: none;
  filter: drop-shadow(0 0 4px rgba(var(--theme-rgb, 79,195,247), 0.55));
}
.lane-hex-cap.top    { top: -4px; }
.lane-hex-cap.bottom { bottom: -4px; }

/* ---- #11 Chromatic aberration on hit ----
   When a card takes damage, its border briefly splits into a
   red-cyan ghosting via two ::before / ::after offsets. 80ms
   pulse, then snaps back. Triggered by adding .hit-chrom class
   (engine code that emits hit events can add this). */
@keyframes hitChromaticAberration {
  0%   { filter: drop-shadow(-3px 0 0 rgba(255,80,80,0.95)) drop-shadow(3px 0 0 rgba(80,200,255,0.95)); }
  60%  { filter: drop-shadow(-1px 0 0 rgba(255,80,80,0.45)) drop-shadow(1px 0 0 rgba(80,200,255,0.45)); }
  100% { filter: none; }
}
.card.hit-chrom { animation: hitChromaticAberration 220ms ease-out; }

/* ---- #15 Light-cycle HP trail ----
   When the .health-bar updates, a thin neon "lightcycle" line
   sweeps across the surface where the new fill ends. Builds on
   the existing .hp-pulse class (already wired via UI). New rule
   adds the leading-edge spike behavior. */
.health-bar.hp-cycle::before {
  content: "";
  position: absolute;
  top: 0; bottom: 0;
  width: 3px;
  background: rgba(255,255,255,0.95);
  box-shadow:
    0 0 8px  rgba(var(--theme-rgb, 79,195,247), 0.85),
    0 0 16px rgba(var(--theme-rgb, 79,195,247), 0.55);
  left: 0;
  animation: hpLightCycle 480ms cubic-bezier(0.22, 1, 0.36, 1) forwards;
  pointer-events: none;
  z-index: 4;
}
@keyframes hpLightCycle {
  0%   { left: 0%;   opacity: 0; }
  20%  { opacity: 1; }
  100% { left: 100%; opacity: 0; }
}

/* ---- #17 Identity-disc arc behind hover-magnified card ----
   When the magnify popup spawns, an arc of light traces around
   the card from one corner. Direct Tron-disc reference. Pure
   CSS via box-shadow + rotating mask. */
@keyframes identityDiscSweep {
  0%   { transform: translate(-50%, -50%) rotate(0deg);   opacity: 0; }
  20%  { opacity: 0.85; }
  100% { transform: translate(-50%, -50%) rotate(360deg); opacity: 0; }
}
.magnify-popup::before {
  content: "";
  position: absolute;
  left: 50%; top: 50%;
  width: 110%; aspect-ratio: 1;
  border-radius: 50%;
  border: 2px solid transparent;
  border-top-color: rgba(var(--theme-rgb, 79,195,247), 0.85);
  border-right-color: rgba(var(--theme-rgb, 79,195,247), 0.30);
  pointer-events: none;
  animation: identityDiscSweep 1.4s linear forwards;
  filter: blur(0.5px) drop-shadow(0 0 8px rgba(var(--theme-rgb, 79,195,247), 0.55));
}

/* ---- #12 Game-over glitch text (one-shot scramble) ----
   The big VICTORY / DEFEAT title gets a 280ms RGB-split scramble
   when it lands, then settles. Triggered by .game-over-glitch
   class on the title element. */
@keyframes gameOverGlitch {
  0%, 100% { transform: translate(0, 0); text-shadow: none; }
  10%  { transform: translate(-2px, 1px); text-shadow: -2px 0 rgba(255,80,80,0.9), 2px 0 rgba(80,200,255,0.9); }
  20%  { transform: translate(2px, -1px);  text-shadow: 2px 0 rgba(255,80,80,0.9), -2px 0 rgba(80,200,255,0.9); }
  35%  { transform: translate(-1px, 0);    text-shadow: -1px 0 rgba(255,80,80,0.7), 1px 0 rgba(80,200,255,0.7); }
  55%  { transform: translate(1px, 0);     text-shadow: 1px 0 rgba(255,80,80,0.5), -1px 0 rgba(80,200,255,0.5); }
}
.game-over-title.glitch { animation: gameOverGlitch 380ms steps(8, end); }

/* ---- #7 Mouse-parallax background grid ----
   The body's existing grid is shifted by --mx / --my custom
   props that ui.js installs on a passive mousemove listener.
   ~5-6px max travel — subtle depth without distraction. */
body {
  --mx: 0px;
  --my: 0px;
}
.background-grid,
body > .grid-bg {
  transform: translate3d(var(--mx, 0), var(--my, 0), 0);
  transition: transform 220ms cubic-bezier(0.2, 0, 0.4, 1);
  will-change: transform;
}

/* ---- #14 Card-play afterimage trail ----
   When a card lands, a 200ms ghost copy of its silhouette stays
   behind, then dissolves. Used by ui.js _spawnPlayAfterimage on
   playCard. */
.card-play-afterimage {
  position: absolute;
  pointer-events: none;
  z-index: 50;
  background: rgba(var(--theme-rgb, 79,195,247), 0.18);
  border: 1px solid rgba(var(--theme-rgb, 79,195,247), 0.55);
  border-radius: 5px;
  filter: blur(1px) drop-shadow(0 0 12px rgba(var(--theme-rgb, 79,195,247), 0.55));
  animation: playAfterimage 380ms ease-out forwards;
}
@keyframes playAfterimage {
  0%   { opacity: 0.7; transform: scale(1);    }
  60%  { opacity: 0.35; transform: scale(1.04); }
  100% { opacity: 0;   transform: scale(1.08); }
}

/* ---- #6 Phase banner refraction ----
   The phase banner text gets a chromatic-aberration split when
   it appears, converging to clean text at ~60% of the banner's
   visible window. Builds on existing .phase-banner.active. */
@keyframes phaseBannerRefract {
  0%   { text-shadow: -3px 0 rgba(255,80,80,0.85), 3px 0 rgba(80,200,255,0.85); }
  60%  { text-shadow: -1px 0 rgba(255,80,80,0.45), 1px 0 rgba(80,200,255,0.45); }
  100% { text-shadow: 0 0 8px rgba(var(--theme-rgb, 79,195,247), 0.65); }
}
.phase-banner.active .phase-banner-text {
  animation: phaseBannerRefract 700ms ease-out;
}

/* ============================================================
   WAVE 1 — "STEP INTO THE GRID" 3D ENVIRONMENT
   ============================================================
   Goal: turn the 2D side-scroller view into a proper 3D scene
   where the player feels like they're STANDING IN the Tron
   grid, looking across the playfield at the opponent.

   Strategy:
     1. #game-area gets a CSS `perspective` so all child 3D
        transforms have a coherent vanishing point.
     2. .board-section (the lane container) tilts back on the X
        axis ~10° so its top edge recedes into the distance —
        enemy lanes feel "further away" than player lanes.
     3. .grid-floor is a new flat plane painted with a Tron
        cyan grid (repeating-linear-gradient), rotated 60° on
        X and pushed back/down so it lays UNDER the lanes and
        recedes toward a horizon.
     4. .board-haze fades the back of the board to soft theme-
        color fog — distance cue used by every 3D game.
     5. .info-bar HUDs get translateZ(40px) so when the board
        tilts they stay flat-on-camera and visibly float above
        the receding playfield (cockpit overlay feel).

   Compositor cost: pure transforms + gradients, GPU-accelerated.
   No layout thrash. Reduced-motion users get a flat 2D fallback
   via the @media block at the bottom.
   ============================================================ */

#game-area {
  /* The vanishing point. Higher = subtler 3D; lower = more
     dramatic. 1500px reads as "moderately deep room" — strong
     enough to feel volumetric, not so strong it warps cards. */
  perspective: 1500px;
  perspective-origin: 50% 75%; /* below the board so player side stays close */
}

/* ---- TRON ARCADE ARENA architecture ------------------------
   User feedback: "lane lines are not correct... stuff behind
   the lanes... a lot of things are off." Root cause: a 3D-
   tilted grid floor intersects the 2D-vertical lane DOM, and
   they geometrically don't belong together. Switching to a
   FLAT arena (the way real Tron lightcycle arenas actually
   look) where depth comes from atmosphere, contrast, and
   card-level 3D — not from perspective tilt of the playfield.
   The arena is now a literal Tron broadcast monitor: bezel
   frame, CRT scanlines, broadcast-carrier stripe, hex texture.
   HUDs sit flat above and below as separate UI panels.
*/
.board-section {
  /* No tilt — flat top-down. Depth lives in the cards (hover
     scale + tilt) and the lane pillars when occupied. */
  transform: none;
  /* overflow:visible — when a board card hovers, it scales 1.4x
     and would otherwise be CLIPPED by overflow:hidden when the
     enlarged top/bottom extends past the section bounds. User
     report: "when you hover over a card on board, It gets cut
     off... the lane cuts it off." The grid-floor that originally
     needed overflow:hidden is now display:none, so we can
     safely allow vertical escape. Side-margin black is preserved
     because nothing inside the section paints into the side
     margins anymore. */
  overflow: visible !important;
  position: relative;
  /* Pure black backdrop — the bezel rectangle the user
     called "distracting" is gone. The arena now reads as a
     boundless black void with the lanes as the only structure
     in it. The only chrome is the subtle motherboard texture. */
  background:
    radial-gradient(circle at 12px 12px,
      rgba(var(--theme-rgb, 46, 204, 113), 0.08) 0.5px,
      transparent 1.5px) 0 0 / 24px 24px,
    #000000 !important;
  /* No border, no box-shadow — bezel removed per user feedback:
     "this little rectangle around the board is kinda distracting.
     I'd rather just this not be in the game at all." */
  border: none !important;
  box-shadow: none !important;
}

/* Grid floor + haze are removed — they were the source of the
   "stuff behind the lanes" problem. The base styles below now
   no-op them in case any leftover DOM sneaks through. */
.board-section .grid-floor,
.board-section .board-haze { display: none !important; }

/* Tron grid floor — fills the board-section, then rotateX(60deg)
   stands it up on its bottom edge so it lays nearly horizontal
   in 3D space. Combined with the parent's rotateX(12deg) and
   #game-area's perspective, the grid lines recede to a vanishing
   point behind the enemy lanes — exactly the Tron POV.
   Painted with two repeating-linear-gradients (one per axis). */
.board-section .grid-floor {
  position: absolute;
  /* Extend BELOW the board so the floor stretches "into the
     distance" past the back wall. Negative top + bigger height
     = floor that exits both edges of the visible board, with
     the perspective transform converging the lines. */
  left: -50%; right: -50%;
  top: 50%;
  height: 1500px;
  pointer-events: none;
  transform: rotateX(70deg);
  transform-origin: top center;
  background:
    /* Vertical lines aligned to the LANE WIDTH (134px). The
       earlier 40px spacing fought visually with the lanes —
       two competing line systems made the back of the playfield
       look noisy. With the grid stride matching lane stride,
       the floor reads as a literal extension of the lane
       boundaries running off into the distance. */
    repeating-linear-gradient(90deg,
      transparent 0,
      transparent 133px,
      rgba(var(--theme-rgb, 46, 204, 113), 0.45) 133px,
      rgba(var(--theme-rgb, 46, 204, 113), 0.45) 134px),
    /* Horizontal lines (perpendicular to viewer, recede with
       perspective). Slightly tighter (90px) so the receding
       lines paint a solid ground texture. */
    repeating-linear-gradient(0deg,
      transparent 0,
      transparent 89px,
      rgba(var(--theme-rgb, 46, 204, 113), 0.30) 89px,
      rgba(var(--theme-rgb, 46, 204, 113), 0.30) 90px),
    /* Subtle theme glow underwash so even the empty cells
       between lines pick up a hint of color */
    radial-gradient(ellipse at center top,
      rgba(var(--theme-rgb, 46, 204, 113), 0.10) 0%,
      transparent 60%);
  /* Fade the floor to black at the far horizon so the lines
     vanish into shadow rather than a hard tile-edge — that's
     the Tron "more grid beyond, lost in the dark" cue. */
  -webkit-mask-image: linear-gradient(180deg,
    rgba(0,0,0,1) 0%,
    rgba(0,0,0,0.75) 25%,
    rgba(0,0,0,0.35) 60%,
    transparent 100%);
          mask-image: linear-gradient(180deg,
    rgba(0,0,0,1) 0%,
    rgba(0,0,0,0.75) 25%,
    rgba(0,0,0,0.35) 60%,
    transparent 100%);
  z-index: 0;
}

/* Atmospheric haze. Applied as an overlay on the board-section
   that fades from clear (near, player side) to soft theme fog
   (far, enemy side). This is THE classic depth cue — distant
   things desaturate and tint toward the ambient sky color. */
.board-section .board-haze {
  position: absolute;
  inset: 0;
  pointer-events: none;
  background: linear-gradient(180deg,
    rgba(var(--theme-rgb, 46, 204, 113), 0.32) 0%,
    rgba(var(--theme-rgb, 46, 204, 113), 0.18) 18%,
    rgba(var(--theme-rgb, 46, 204, 113), 0.06) 40%,
    transparent 65%);
  z-index: 1;
  /* Add a subtle edge vignette to deepen the "you're inside a
     room looking down a corridor" feel — corners darken slightly. */
  box-shadow: inset 0 0 80px 0 rgba(0, 0, 0, 0.6);
  /* Stays parallel to the lanes so when the board tilts, the
     haze tilts with it — the "far" of the board IS the foggy
     part. */
}

/* HUDs are flat UI panels — they live OUTSIDE the 3D arena
   like the bezel housing of a TV. No transforms; they sit
   above and below the arena window as separate, clean panels.
   z-index raised to 210 (was 5) so the Tron cursor-trail SVG
   (#tron-cursor-trail z-index 200) can't paint cyan over the
   HP bar / block circle / energy diamond via its
   mix-blend-mode: screen. User report 2026-05-18: "encroaching
   color, when I have more blue than red, there's like a little
   blue section on their health bar." */
#game-area .info-bar.ai-bar,
#game-area .info-bar.player-bar {
  position: relative;
  z-index: 210;
}
/* HP bar perimeter pulse — DISABLED 2026-05-18 per user feedback:
   "the animations are still running, so those need to be killed
   because I don't want too many animations going because that's
   what's gonna slow down the game on mobile."
   The tronTubePulse on .health-bar::after was animating border-
   color + box-shadow every 6s. Even at the lower 50% alpha peak
   this rendered as a continually brightening rim around both HP
   bars — perceived as the bar "glowing in/out" which the user
   wanted off. Border + box-shadow stay (the bars still have a
   static red/cyan rim), just no longer pulsed. */
.info-bar .health-bar.tron-perimeter::after {
  animation: none !important;
}

/* The lanes need to render IN FRONT of the floor + haze. */
.board-section #board {
  position: relative;
  z-index: 2;
}

/* User vision: "Story through colors. White = neutral, player
   theme = ally only, enemy theme = enemy only, color-mix blend
   for contested. Color theory: green+red→yellow. Different
   themes blend to different colors — purple+red different from
   blue+red different from green+red."

   Implementation: the lane border is painted via a 6-stop linear
   gradient that always shows:
     0–3%   = enemy red (the persistent TOP TICK)
     9–91%  = --lane-mid-color (the OCCUPANCY signal)
     97–100% = player theme (the persistent BOTTOM TICK)
   Single CSS variable --lane-mid-color drives the middle band.
   Default is white (empty/neutral), occupancy classes redefine
   it. Contested lanes use color-mix() for true light blending. */
.board-section .lane {
  /* Empty/neutral lane — uses the SPECIAL tier white from the
     canonical rarity color system (cost-7/8 cards). User spec:
     "the white I use is the same as the seven cost and eight
     cost cards white." Single source of truth for the whole
     game's "neutral" color. */
  --lane-mid-color: rgba(240, 245, 250, 0.85);
  border-image: linear-gradient(180deg,
      rgba(var(--enemy-r, 231), var(--enemy-g, 76), var(--enemy-b, 60), 0.95)   0%,
      rgba(var(--enemy-r, 231), var(--enemy-g, 76), var(--enemy-b, 60), 0.95)   3%,
      var(--lane-mid-color) 9%,
      var(--lane-mid-color) 91%,
      rgba(var(--player-r, 79), var(--player-g, 195), var(--player-b, 247), 0.95) 97%,
      rgba(var(--player-r, 79), var(--player-g, 195), var(--player-b, 247), 0.95) 100%
    ) 1 !important;
  border-width: 1px !important;
  border-style: solid !important;
  box-shadow: none !important;
}

/* Ally only — middle band is the player theme color. */
.board-section .lane.occ-player {
  --lane-mid-color: rgba(var(--player-r, 79), var(--player-g, 195), var(--player-b, 247), 0.85);
}

/* Enemy only — middle band is enemy red. */
.board-section .lane.occ-ai {
  --lane-mid-color: rgba(var(--enemy-r, 231), var(--enemy-g, 76), var(--enemy-b, 60), 0.85);
}

/* Contested — the BORDER stays in three discrete zones with
   HARD edges (no blending). Top half = enemy red. Middle thin
   band = LEGENDARY GOLD (rgba(255,215,0,0.95)) — the canonical
   rarity color from the cost-9/10 cards. User spec: "this
   yellow should be the same yellow you use as in legendaries.
   Like, just how they were perfect, but I would like the
   legendaries... that same legendary middle, like, the same
   gradient behind a legendary card. For the middle." Single
   source of truth across the game's color vocabulary. */
.board-section .lane.occ-both {
  --lane-mid-color: rgba(255, 215, 0, 0.95);
  border-image: linear-gradient(180deg,
    rgba(var(--enemy-r, 231), var(--enemy-g, 76), var(--enemy-b, 60), 0.95)   0%,
    rgba(var(--enemy-r, 231), var(--enemy-g, 76), var(--enemy-b, 60), 0.95)   46%,
    var(--lane-mid-color) 47%,
    var(--lane-mid-color) 53%,
    rgba(var(--player-r, 79), var(--player-g, 195), var(--player-b, 247), 0.95) 54%,
    rgba(var(--player-r, 79), var(--player-g, 195), var(--player-b, 247), 0.95) 100%
  ) 1 !important;
}

/* Hover — brighten the middle band to the player theme color
   (the "click to play" affordance). Inset glow only — no outer
   bleed so neighboring lanes don't pick up false highlight. */
.board-section .lane:hover {
  --lane-mid-color: rgba(var(--player-r, 79), var(--player-g, 195), var(--player-b, 247), 0.95);
  box-shadow:
    inset 0 0 16px rgba(var(--player-r, 79), var(--player-g, 195), var(--player-b, 247), 0.18) !important;
}

/* ============================================================
   WAVE 2 — LANE VOLUME (cards stop being flat stickers)
   ============================================================ */

/* #5 — VOLUMETRIC LANE LIGHT PILLAR
   Mirrors the --lane-mid-color border system above. A subtle
   inner glow in the same occupancy color so the lane reads as
   "filled with this color's energy" — not just outlined.
   For contested (occ-both) lanes, color-mix() blends the two
   theme colors so the pillar IS the contested-blend visual
   (e.g., yellow for green+red). Story-through-color in 3D. */
.lane.occ-ai::before,
.lane.occ-player::before,
.lane.occ-both::before {
  content: '';
  position: absolute;
  inset: 0;
  pointer-events: none;
  z-index: 0;
  /* Mid-band soft glow + edge lighting using the same
     --lane-mid-color the border-image uses. The fade-to-
     transparent at top/bottom keeps the persistent enemy/player
     ticks (painted by border-image) the strongest signal at
     the edges. */
  background: linear-gradient(180deg,
    transparent 0%,
    var(--pillar-tint, transparent) 22%,
    var(--pillar-tint, transparent) 78%,
    transparent 100%);
  box-shadow:
    inset 18px 0 30px -20px var(--pillar-edge, transparent),
    inset -18px 0 30px -20px var(--pillar-edge, transparent);
}
.lane.occ-ai::before {
  --pillar-tint: rgba(var(--enemy-r, 231), var(--enemy-g, 76), var(--enemy-b, 60), 0.10);
  --pillar-edge: rgba(var(--enemy-r, 231), var(--enemy-g, 76), var(--enemy-b, 60), 0.28);
}
.lane.occ-player::before {
  --pillar-tint: rgba(var(--player-r, 79), var(--player-g, 195), var(--player-b, 247), 0.10);
  --pillar-edge: rgba(var(--player-r, 79), var(--player-g, 195), var(--player-b, 247), 0.28);
}
/* Contested pillar — RED top half (behind AI card) → tiny GOLD
   tint in the middle (where cards meet) → GREEN bottom half
   (behind player card). The gold appears ONLY on the lane edges
   (border-image, defined above) — the lane interior just shows
   subtle red and green halves with no center beam. User spec:
   "scrap [the gold halo band] idea... make it look regular,
   just gold." The border-image gold band IS the gold signal;
   the interior doesn't need to repeat it. */
.lane.occ-both::before {
  background: linear-gradient(180deg,
    transparent 0%,
    rgba(var(--enemy-r, 231), var(--enemy-g, 76), var(--enemy-b, 60), 0.10) 22%,
    rgba(var(--enemy-r, 231), var(--enemy-g, 76), var(--enemy-b, 60), 0.10) 46%,
    transparent 50%,
    rgba(var(--player-r, 79), var(--player-g, 195), var(--player-b, 247), 0.10) 54%,
    rgba(var(--player-r, 79), var(--player-g, 195), var(--player-b, 247), 0.10) 78%,
    transparent 100%);
  box-shadow:
    inset 18px 0 30px -20px rgba(var(--enemy-r, 231), var(--enemy-g, 76), var(--enemy-b, 60), 0.28),
    inset -18px 0 30px -20px rgba(var(--enemy-r, 231), var(--enemy-g, 76), var(--enemy-b, 60), 0.28),
    inset 0 18px 30px -20px rgba(var(--enemy-r, 231), var(--enemy-g, 76), var(--enemy-b, 60), 0.18),
    inset 0 -18px 30px -20px rgba(var(--player-r, 79), var(--player-g, 195), var(--player-b, 247), 0.18);
}

/* #6 — CARD FLOOR SHADOWS
   Each card on the board casts a soft elliptical shadow on the
   grid plane beneath it. Implemented as `::after` with rotateX
   to lay it nearly flat, with a strong blur so it reads as a
   diffuse contact shadow rather than a hard projection.
   Anchored to the card's BOTTOM so when the playfield tilts,
   the shadow stays visually below the card.
   This sells "the card is a physical token resting on the
   table" rather than "a sticker pasted to the surface." */
.card.ally-card::after,
.card.enemy-card::after {
  content: '';
  position: absolute;
  bottom: -12px;
  left: 8%;
  right: 8%;
  height: 28px;
  pointer-events: none;
  background: radial-gradient(ellipse at center,
    rgba(0,0,0,0.55) 0%,
    rgba(0,0,0,0.30) 40%,
    transparent 75%);
  filter: blur(4px);
  z-index: -1;
  /* Lay the shadow flat onto the floor plane. The board tilts
     forward 10°; the floor under it is rotateX(70°) effectively.
     This shadow tilts roughly with the floor so it appears to
     rest on the same plane. */
  transform: rotateX(60deg);
  transform-origin: top center;
  /* Pulse subtly with the lane's living chrome. */
  opacity: 0.85;
}

/* #7 — ON-PLAY FLOOR RIPPLE (enhance existing card-landing-ring)
   The existing card-landing-ring expands as a vertical ring on
   the card surface. Re-orient it to lay on the FLOOR plane so
   the ripple radiates outward across the grid like a data-pulse,
   not floats up off the card. rotateX(60deg) drops it onto the
   floor; the existing scale-and-fade animation does the rest. */
.card-landing-ring {
  transform: translate(-50%, -50%) rotateX(60deg) !important;
  transform-origin: center center;
}

/* ============================================================
   WAVE 3 — CAMERA + IMMERSION POLISH
   ============================================================ */

/* #8 — MOUSE-PARALLAX CAMERA FOLLOW
   The cursor's distance from screen center drives a tiny
   rotateY/X on the entire #game-area, so the whole scene
   pivots subtly as you "look around." Compose with the
   existing perspective for a real "I'm in this room" feel.
   The actual transform values are JS-driven via CSS vars
   --cam-tx (-1..1) / --cam-ty (-1..1), set by installCameraParallax(). */
#game-area {
  /* `transform` here composes with the parent perspective —
     since we're a 3D context, this rotation becomes a real
     camera pivot, not a 2D rotation. Magnitudes are tiny
     (±2°) so the playfield doesn't feel seasick. */
  transform: rotateY(calc(var(--cam-tx, 0) * 2deg))
             rotateX(calc(var(--cam-ty, 0) * -1.5deg));
  transform-origin: center bottom;
  transition: transform 220ms cubic-bezier(0.22, 1, 0.36, 1);
}

/* #9 — PARTICLES IN TRUE 3D SPACE
   The existing .game-particle divs got a translateZ via a CSS
   variable --pz set by JS (0 if not assigned). Combined with
   the parent's 3D perspective, particles at different Z
   distances move at different apparent rates when the
   camera-parallax pivots — actual stereoscopic depth, not
   layered 2D parallax. Also distance-fade so the deepest
   particles dim toward the haze color. */
.game-particle {
  transform: translateZ(var(--pz, 0px));
  /* Far particles dim, near ones stay bright. */
  opacity: calc(0.6 + 0.4 * (1 - abs(var(--pz, 0px) / 250px)));
}

/* #11 — DEEPER CARD INTERIOR Z-LAYERS (push current values further)
   Earlier the card hover parallax shifted content layers at
   4-24px depths. Bumping the deepest layers (cost orb, stat
   circles) pushes the "card holds a miniature 3D world" feel
   further — when tilted, you visibly see PAST the card body. */
.hand-card-wrapper:hover .card.hand-card .card-cost {
  transform: translateZ(40px) !important;
}
.hand-card-wrapper:hover .card.hand-card .stat-circle {
  transform: translateZ(28px) !important;
}
.hand-card-wrapper:hover .card.hand-card .status-badges {
  transform: translateZ(20px) !important;
}
.hand-card-wrapper:hover .card.hand-card .card-name-banner {
  transform: translateZ(12px) !important;
}
.hand-card-wrapper:hover .card.hand-card .card-desc {
  transform: translateZ(6px) !important;
}

/* ============================================================
   TRON RETRO POLISH — A, B, D from the suggestion list
   ============================================================ */

/* Polish A — CRT SCANLINES on the arena ONLY. Subtle horizontal
   scan lines drift slowly down the arena, simulating an old
   CRT broadcasting the Tron signal. Pure decoration; never
   eats clicks. The animation moves at 12s per cycle so it's
   ambient, not distracting. Stays inside .board-section's
   overflow:hidden so it never bleeds into HUDs.
   Lives as a dedicated DIV (not ::before) because ::before is
   reserved by the existing round-transition sweep system. */
.board-section .crt-scanlines {
  position: absolute;
  inset: 0;
  pointer-events: none;
  z-index: 6;
  background:
    /* Two-line scanline pattern at 3px stride */
    repeating-linear-gradient(0deg,
      transparent 0,
      transparent 2px,
      rgba(255, 255, 255, 0.025) 2px,
      rgba(255, 255, 255, 0.025) 3px);
  /* Slow vertical drift — CRT raster scan feel */
  animation: crtScanDrift 12s linear infinite;
  /* Subtle screen-blend so the lines glow rather than darken */
  mix-blend-mode: screen;
}
@keyframes crtScanDrift {
  0%   { background-position: 0 0; }
  100% { background-position: 0 60px; }
}

/* (Carrier-stripe removed per user feedback: "a bunch of green
   on top below the enemy health bar... a lot of extra stuff."
   It was the prominent green horizontal beam at the top of the
   arena. Cleaner without it.) */
.board-section .carrier-stripe { display: none !important; }

/* Polish D — BOOT-UP SCAN. Bright horizontal scan line travels
   top→bottom across the arena once at round start, like a CRT
   powering on. Default state: invisible. JS adds .boot-scan
   class to .board-section to trigger the 1.2s animation on
   the inner .boot-scan-beam element. */
.board-section .boot-scan-beam {
  position: absolute;
  left: 0; right: 0;
  top: 0;
  height: 4px;
  background: linear-gradient(90deg,
    transparent 0%,
    rgba(var(--theme-rgb, 46, 204, 113), 1) 50%,
    transparent 100%);
  box-shadow:
    0 0 16px rgba(var(--theme-rgb, 46, 204, 113), 0.95),
    0 0 32px rgba(var(--theme-rgb, 46, 204, 113), 0.55),
    0 0 64px rgba(var(--theme-rgb, 46, 204, 113), 0.30);
  pointer-events: none;
  z-index: 7;
  opacity: 0;
}
.board-section.boot-scan .boot-scan-beam {
  animation: bootScan 1.2s cubic-bezier(0.4, 0, 0.6, 1) forwards;
}
@keyframes bootScan {
  0%   { top: 0;    opacity: 0; }
  10%  { opacity: 1; }
  85%  { opacity: 1; }
  100% { top: 100%; opacity: 0; }
}

/* ============================================================
   COLOR INVASION — winning side's color bleeds into the loser's
   UI as the game progresses. Vars come from JS via
   _updateDominanceVars() each render. Smooth 800ms transitions
   make changes feel atmospheric, not flashy.
     --player-dominance : 0..1  (how much player leads in lane control)
     --enemy-dominance  : 0..1  (how much enemy leads)
   At most one is >0 at a time; the other is 0. "Confident"
   intensity = 50% mix at full dominance. User can dial up/down.
   ============================================================ */

/* COLOR INVASION — HARD-EDGE LINE CONVERSION
   User spec: "instead of having, like, this weird green overlay,
   I would rather it just the red starts to turn into green...
   some of the lines start to turn green. I never want green and
   red to make, like, go over one another or any color for that
   matter. I want there to be distinct lines when the color
   starts and stops."
   Replaces the previous color-mix overlay (which BLENDED red→
   olive→green) with a HARD-STOP linear-gradient on the border-
   image. The LEFT N% of the border is literally PLAYER COLOR.
   The RIGHT (100-N)% is literally ENEMY RED. There's a hard cut
   between them — no blending, no overlay, no muddy middle.
   Capped at 60% conversion so the loser's HUD always retains
   identity color on the right edge. */
.info-bar.ai-bar {
  /* The dominance position determines where the hard color cut
     happens. At dominance 0.6 → cut at 60% (LEFT 60% green,
     RIGHT 40% red). */
  --inv-stop: calc(var(--player-dominance, 0) * 60%);
  border-image-source: linear-gradient(90deg,
    rgba(var(--player-r, 79), var(--player-g, 195), var(--player-b, 247), 0.95) 0%,
    rgba(var(--player-r, 79), var(--player-g, 195), var(--player-b, 247), 0.95) var(--inv-stop),
    rgba(var(--enemy-r, 231), var(--enemy-g, 76), var(--enemy-b, 60), 0.95) var(--inv-stop),
    rgba(var(--enemy-r, 231), var(--enemy-g, 76), var(--enemy-b, 60), 0.95) 100%) !important;
  border-image-slice: 1 !important;
  /* Halo: weakens proportionally as dominance grows so the
     invaded HUD literally dims as its lines convert. No green
     glow added — the line conversion IS the signal. */
  box-shadow:
    0 0 6px rgba(var(--enemy-r, 231), var(--enemy-g, 76), var(--enemy-b, 60),
      calc(0.45 * (1 - var(--player-dominance, 0) * 0.6))),
    0 0 12px rgba(var(--enemy-r, 231), var(--enemy-g, 76), var(--enemy-b, 60),
      calc(0.22 * (1 - var(--player-dominance, 0) * 0.6))) !important;
  transition: border-image-source 800ms ease-out, box-shadow 800ms ease-out;
}

.info-bar.player-bar {
  --inv-stop: calc(var(--enemy-dominance, 0) * 60%);
  border-image-source: linear-gradient(90deg,
    rgba(var(--enemy-r, 231), var(--enemy-g, 76), var(--enemy-b, 60), 0.95) 0%,
    rgba(var(--enemy-r, 231), var(--enemy-g, 76), var(--enemy-b, 60), 0.95) var(--inv-stop),
    rgba(var(--player-r, 79), var(--player-g, 195), var(--player-b, 247), 0.95) var(--inv-stop),
    rgba(var(--player-r, 79), var(--player-g, 195), var(--player-b, 247), 0.95) 100%) !important;
  border-image-slice: 1 !important;
  box-shadow:
    0 0 6px rgba(var(--player-r, 79), var(--player-g, 195), var(--player-b, 247),
      calc(0.30 * (1 - var(--enemy-dominance, 0) * 0.6))),
    0 0 12px rgba(var(--player-r, 79), var(--player-g, 195), var(--player-b, 247),
      calc(0.18 * (1 - var(--enemy-dominance, 0) * 0.6))) !important;
  transition: border-image-source 800ms ease-out, box-shadow 800ms ease-out;
}

/* HUD content: only .bar-cell children need z-index promotion
   (NOT the SVG .ai-thinking-ring — see grid-row phantom-row bug
   from earlier). */
.info-bar > .bar-cell { position: relative; z-index: 2; }

/* EMPTY LANE BIAS — empty lanes shift slightly toward the
   dominant side's color. At full dominance, white lanes pick
   up a 12% color bias. Subtle but cumulative — a board with
   4-of-6 player lanes will have its 2 empties subtly green-
   tinted, a board you're losing has empties red-tinted. */
.board-section .lane.occ-none {
  --lane-mid-color: color-mix(in srgb,
    rgba(255, 255, 255, 0.30) calc(100% - (var(--player-dominance, 0) + var(--enemy-dominance, 0)) * 12%),
    color-mix(in srgb,
      rgba(var(--player-r, 79), var(--player-g, 195), var(--player-b, 247), 0.30)
        calc(50% + (var(--player-dominance, 0) - var(--enemy-dominance, 0)) * 50%),
      rgba(var(--enemy-r, 231), var(--enemy-g, 76), var(--enemy-b, 60), 0.30)
        calc(50% - (var(--player-dominance, 0) - var(--enemy-dominance, 0)) * 50%))
    calc((var(--player-dominance, 0) + var(--enemy-dominance, 0)) * 12%));
  transition: border-image 800ms ease-out;
}

/* Reduced-motion users get the old flat 2D view. The 3D
   perspective tilt can trigger motion-sensitivity issues, so
   we honor the OS preference. */
@media (prefers-reduced-motion: reduce) {
  #game-area {
    perspective: none;
    transform: none !important;
  }
  .board-section {
    transform: none;
    transform-style: flat;
    border: 1px solid rgba(var(--theme-rgb, 46, 204, 113), 0.6);
    box-shadow: none;
  }
  .board-section .crt-scanlines { display: none; }
  .board-section .boot-scan-beam { display: none; }
  .board-section .carrier-stripe { animation: none; }
  #game-area .info-bar.ai-bar,
  #game-area .info-bar.player-bar { transform: none; }
  .lane.occ-ai::before,
  .lane.occ-player::before,
  .lane.occ-both::before { display: none; }
  .card.ally-card::after,
  .card.enemy-card::after { display: none; }
  .card-landing-ring { transform: translate(-50%, -50%) !important; }
  .game-particle { transform: none; }
}

/* ============================================================
   SANDBOX (dev playtest panel) — floating overlay with a
   card-search field, action buttons, and a click-to-spawn list.
   Triggered by main-menu Sandbox option OR `~` hotkey in-game.
   ============================================================ */
.sandbox-overlay {
  position: fixed;
  top: 80px; right: 16px;
  width: 340px; max-height: 80vh;
  z-index: 200;
  display: none;
  font-family: 'Rajdhani', sans-serif;
}
.sandbox-overlay[style*="display: flex"] { display: flex !important; }
.sandbox-panel {
  width: 100%;
  background: #060a12;
  border: 1px solid rgba(var(--theme-rgb, 46, 204, 113), 0.85);
  border-radius: 6px;
  box-shadow:
    0 0 12px rgba(var(--theme-rgb, 46, 204, 113), 0.45),
    0 0 24px rgba(var(--theme-rgb, 46, 204, 113), 0.18),
    0 8px 32px rgba(0, 0, 0, 0.75);
  display: flex; flex-direction: column;
  overflow: hidden;
}
.sandbox-header {
  display: flex; justify-content: space-between; align-items: center;
  padding: 10px 14px;
  border-bottom: 1px solid rgba(var(--theme-rgb, 46, 204, 113), 0.45);
  background: linear-gradient(180deg, rgba(var(--theme-rgb, 46, 204, 113), 0.10), rgba(var(--theme-rgb, 46, 204, 113), 0));
}
.sandbox-title {
  font-weight: 700; letter-spacing: 3px;
  color: rgb(var(--theme-rgb, 46, 204, 113));
  text-shadow: 0 0 8px rgba(var(--theme-rgb, 46, 204, 113), 0.6);
}
.sandbox-close {
  background: transparent; border: none; cursor: pointer;
  font-size: 22px; line-height: 1;
  color: rgba(var(--theme-rgb, 46, 204, 113), 0.85);
}
.sandbox-close:hover { color: #fff; }
.sandbox-controls {
  display: grid; grid-template-columns: 1fr 1fr; gap: 6px;
  padding: 8px 12px;
  border-bottom: 1px solid rgba(var(--theme-rgb, 46, 204, 113), 0.25);
}
.sandbox-btn {
  background: rgba(var(--theme-rgb, 46, 204, 113), 0.10);
  border: 1px solid rgba(var(--theme-rgb, 46, 204, 113), 0.55);
  color: #d6e3eb;
  font-family: inherit;
  font-size: 12px; font-weight: 600;
  letter-spacing: 1px;
  padding: 8px 10px;
  cursor: pointer;
  border-radius: 3px;
  transition: background 120ms;
}
.sandbox-btn:hover {
  background: rgba(var(--theme-rgb, 46, 204, 113), 0.25);
}
.sandbox-search {
  margin: 8px 12px 6px;
  padding: 8px 10px;
  background: #0a0e16;
  border: 1px solid rgba(var(--theme-rgb, 46, 204, 113), 0.45);
  color: #f0f6fb;
  font-family: inherit;
  font-size: 13px;
  border-radius: 3px;
}
.sandbox-search:focus {
  outline: none;
  border-color: rgba(var(--theme-rgb, 46, 204, 113), 0.95);
  box-shadow: 0 0 6px rgba(var(--theme-rgb, 46, 204, 113), 0.55);
}
.sandbox-list {
  flex: 1 1 auto;
  overflow-y: auto;
  padding: 4px 12px 12px;
  max-height: 50vh;
  font-size: 12px;
}
.sb-row {
  display: grid;
  grid-template-columns: 24px 1fr 50px 40px;
  gap: 8px;
  align-items: center;
  padding: 6px 8px;
  border-radius: 3px;
  cursor: pointer;
  transition: background 100ms;
  border: 1px solid transparent;
}
.sb-row:hover {
  background: rgba(var(--theme-rgb, 46, 204, 113), 0.10);
  border-color: rgba(var(--theme-rgb, 46, 204, 113), 0.40);
}
.sb-cost {
  width: 22px; height: 22px;
  display: inline-flex; justify-content: center; align-items: center;
  border: 1px solid rgba(var(--theme-rgb, 46, 204, 113), 0.65);
  border-radius: 50%;
  font-weight: 700;
  background: #000;
  color: rgba(var(--theme-rgb, 46, 204, 113), 1);
}
.sb-name {
  color: #e6ecf2;
  font-weight: 600;
  letter-spacing: 0.5px;
}
.sb-stats {
  color: rgba(255, 255, 255, 0.55);
  font-variant-numeric: tabular-nums;
  text-align: right;
}
.sb-kind {
  color: rgba(155, 89, 182, 0.85);
  font-size: 10px;
  text-transform: uppercase;
  letter-spacing: 1.5px;
  text-align: right;
}
.sb-row.sb-trick .sb-cost {
  border-color: rgba(155, 89, 182, 0.65);
  color: rgba(217, 165, 255, 1);
}
.sb-empty {
